 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 666915,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Suppose another <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">__init__()</code> definition is added after the first with the header as follows:\n<pre class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">def __init__(self, p_id, bp, tmp, hr, rr)</pre>\nWhat is the impact on the program?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1329754,
     "content_html": "no change.",
     "correctness": "0.0",
     "feedback_html": "The second definition is allowed but overwrites the definition of __init__()."
    },
    {
     "id": 1329755,
     "content_html": "Second <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">__init__()</code> definition produces an error.",
     "correctness": "0.0",
     "feedback_html": "The second definition doesn't immediately produce an error, but the definition overwrites __init__()."
    },
    {
     "id": 1329756,
     "content_html": "First two <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">__init__()</code> calls produce an error.",
     "correctness": "1.0",
     "feedback_html": "The second definition overwrites the first as multiple explicit definitions of <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">__init__()</code> aren't allowed. <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">__init__()</code> requires all parameters, so the first two <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">__init__()</code> calls for <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">patient_1</code> and <code class=\"python\" data-lang=\"python\">patient_2</code> are incorrect."
    }
   ],
